# UX/UI Portfolio Creation Rule

**Full canonical rule:** `ux-ui-portfolio-creation-rule.md` (repo root, 766 lines). Read and follow it in full whenever creating or editing portfolio copy, case study pages, Sanity case study content, or work listing data.

---

## Repo-specific notes

- Case study pages live at `src/app/work/[slug]/page.tsx`.
- Use `src/app/work/devrev/page.tsx` as the **structural reference** for full-section case studies (role scope, challenge, process steps, design decisions, outcomes, reflection).
- Do **not** model new case studies after `src/app/work/soundscope/page.tsx` — it contains invented outcome percentages that violate the no-fake-metrics rule.
- Project data lives in `src/lib/data.ts`; which slugs have live routes is controlled by `src/lib/caseStudyRoutes.ts`.
- Whenever you add a new case study page, also append its slug to `CASE_STUDY_SLUGS` in `src/lib/caseStudyRoutes.ts` so the tile becomes linkable.

---

## Always-enforce checklist (summary of canonical rule)

Before shipping any case study page, Sanity document, or work listing copy, confirm every item:

**Content integrity**
- No fake metrics, fake percentages, or invented quantitative outcomes unless exact data is provided in source material
- No fake user quotes, client testimonials, research findings, or business results
- No fake validation claims ("validated with 100 users", "the client reported major revenue growth")
- Inferred context labeled explicitly: "Based on the available screens…", "The design approach likely focused on…"
- Every senior claim connects to a visible UX problem, design decision, system improvement, or handoff improvement

**Case study structure**
- 60-Second Summary at the top (Product / Problem / My role / Main UX challenge / Key design decision / System contribution / AI-dev collaboration / Outcome)
- Project context, My role (specific — not "I worked on the design"), Challenge, Process, Design Decisions, Design System, AI/Dev Collaboration, Screens, Outcome, Reflection
- Design decisions use Decision / Why / Result blocks
- Outcome is qualitative unless exact numbers are provided
- Reflection ends each case study: honest, forward-looking, not self-promotional

**UX problem-solving**
- Identify relevant problems: unclear entry points, weak hierarchy, inconsistent patterns, missing states, poor handoff, complex data, design system drift
- Use design rationale phrasing, not proof phrasing ("the flow was structured to reduce ambiguity" not "reduced task time by 60%")
- AI presented as workflow amplifier (requirements structuring, handoff notes, implementation alignment) — never as the designer

**Visual evidence**
- Screenshots are evidence, not decoration — each must answer: what does it show, why is it important, what decision does it support
- Do not embed weak, outdated, or compressed images; rebuild as live portfolio components instead
- Curate for storytelling; do not dump all available screens

**UI consistency**
- Same grid, type scale, spacing, color logic, card components, badges, CTAs, image framing across all pages
- No one-off visual treatments per project
- Portfolio reads as one coherent product

**Writing style**
- No: "crafted delightful experiences", "pixel-perfect magic", "passionate designer", empty buzzwords
- Yes: clear reasoning, concrete examples, short paragraphs, business/user/design balance
- Rewrite generics into specifics: "I restructured the dashboard around clearer information hierarchy…" not "I redesigned the dashboard."

**Developer handoff (when relevant)**
- Show implementation-aware decisions: component references, state logic, token usage, edge cases, Figma comments, implementation notes
- Case studies prove design work does not stop at mockups

**Developer collaboration section (when relevant)**
- Explain how AI was used: structuring requirements, comparing design intent against implementation, preparing handoff notes, reducing ambiguity
- Never write that AI made the design

